# Example keyboard_joystick mapping file (keyboard_joystick -c <file>).
#
# Every virtual joystick presents itself as a wired Xbox 360 controller,
# so the A/B/X/Y names below are the letters printed on an Xbox pad and
# shown by SDL, Steam and games.
#
# One rule per line:
#   <joystick> <key> <target>
#
#   <joystick>  joystick number, starting at 1. The highest number used
#               decides how many virtual joysticks are created.
#   <key>       key name, case-insensitive, optional KEY_ prefix
#               (W, w, KEY_W, LEFTSHIFT, BACKSLASH, KP1, F1, ...).
#   <target>    an axis direction:
#                 LEFT RIGHT UP DOWN            left stick
#                 RLEFT RRIGHT RUP RDOWN        right stick
#                 DPLEFT DPRIGHT DPUP DPDOWN    d-pad
#                 LT RT                         analogue triggers
#               or a button, BTN_ prefix optional:
#                 A B X Y TL TR SELECT START MODE THUMBL THUMBR
#               (LB, RB, BACK, GUIDE, LS and RS are accepted too).
#
# Blank lines and '#' comments are ignored. Several keys may drive the
# same target, and one key may drive several targets. A file replaces
# the built-in mapping entirely, so list every rule you want.
#
# This example reproduces the built-in default mapping; dump the
# effective one at any time with "keyboard_joystick --print-map".

# joystick 1: WASD to move, T/F/G/H diamond laid out like the face
# buttons of an Xbox pad (T on top is Y, F on the left is X)
1 W            UP
1 S            DOWN
1 A            LEFT
1 D            RIGHT
1 G            A
1 H            B
1 F            X
1 T            Y

# joystick 2: arrow keys to move, Ins/Home/PgUp/ScLk/End cluster for the
# buttons, in the same diamond around Home, which is the START button
2 UP           UP
2 DOWN         DOWN
2 LEFT         LEFT
2 RIGHT        RIGHT
2 END          A
2 PAGEUP       B
2 INSERT       X
2 SCROLLLOCK   Y
2 HOME         START
